Choose the API for your language. The Zig walkthrough builds one complete
CapTP call; the C ABI and language bindings provide the same operations with
their own ownership and concurrency conventions.

- <a href="#in-zig">Zig</a>
- <a href="#from-c-or-any-ffi-host">C or another FFI host</a>
- [Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust'))
- [TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts'))
- [Python]($link.page('bindings/python'))
- [Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket'))
- [Browser WebAssembly]($link.page('bindings/browser'))

pancake is not released yet. Pin a commit when you add it to a project; the
public API can still change.

## In Zig

pancake is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) package. Add a pinned checkout to
your build:

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake#COMMIT_SHA
```

Replace `COMMIT_SHA` with the revision you want to pin.

```zig
const pancake = b.dependency("pancake", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
exe.root_module.addImport("pancake", pancake.module("pancake"));
```

## Dial

The **tcp** tab is the copy-paste first call: a cleartext `host:port`, the same
shape as the README. It authenticates nobody; use it to learn the API. For a
deployment, pick a row from [Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing'))
and switch tabs. The snippets take `io` and an allocator; create `io` from
`std.Io.Threaded`:

```zig
var threaded = std.Io.Threaded.init(allocator, .{});
const io = threaded.io();
```

