> **tcp**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
>
> pub fn example(io: std.Io, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !void {
>     // Plain TCP uses a cleartext `host:port` locator.
>     var tcp_resident = pancake.net.tcp.Resident{ .io = io };
>     const netlayers = [_]pancake.net.netlayer.Netlayer{tcp_resident.netlayer()};
>     const partner = pancake.net.tcp.locator("127.0.0.1:9099");
> ```
> **inproc**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
>
> pub fn example(
>     allocator: std.mem.Allocator,
>     network: *pancake.net.inproc.Network,
> ) !void {
>     // The listening peer and dialer share this process-local switchboard.
>     const netlayers = [_]pancake.net.netlayer.Netlayer{network.netlayer()};
>     const partner = pancake.net.inproc.locator("greeter");
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```zig
    const session = try pancake.driver.connect(
        allocator,
        &netlayers,
        partner,
        .ephemeral,
        .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
    );
    defer session.deinit();

    const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability this peer published
    var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
    defer arena.deinit();
    const settled = try session.call(greeter, "greet", &.{.{ .string = "world" }}, arena.allocator());
    switch (settled) {
        .fulfilled => |value| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{value.string}),
        .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
    }
}
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:zig -->
```

The CapTP operations stay the same across transports. What changes is the
locator, the resident, and whether the channel is cleartext or sealed.

On the tcp path the locator is a `host:port` such as `"127.0.0.1:9099"`.
On the `keyed-tcp` and `noise-tcp` paths the designator is the peer's durable
key, and the bound address rides in the `direct-addresses` hint instead. The
two timeout values keep network waits finite.

`.ephemeral` is the session identity, a throwaway keypair minted for this
connection. The arena owns reply allocations; dropping it frees them.

See the complete client and server in
[`01_connect_and_serve.zig`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig).
`keyed-tcp` keeps CapTP cleartext with a key designator; `noise-tcp` seals the
same sockets with Noise_XK. The [Noise guide]($link.page('transports/noise'))
covers when to pick Noise over iroh and how `noise-relay` seals the relay path.
The iroh tab uses a node-id locator; the [iroh guide]($link.page('transports/iroh'))
covers discovery and deployment.

## Listen

Each tab is the listen half of the dial example above: bind, publish a greeter,
accept, and serve.

